cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Tether in your research, please cite it using this metadata."
title: "Tether"
abstract: >-
  A cross-platform, provenance-first desktop suite for single-molecule FRET
  (smFRET) analysis: native movie-to-trace extraction, a trace-to-movie
  round-trip browser, per-condition machine-assisted curation, photophysical
  corrections, and HMM idealization via an isolated tMAVEN sidecar. Built for
  the Mondragón Lab, Northwestern University.
type: software
# Named people first: GitHub's "Cite this repository" button, Zenodo and JOSS all
# require identifiable human authors — an entity-only author list credits nobody.
# "The Tether Authors" is retained below as an additional entity credit because it
# is the copyright holder in NOTICE, REUSE.toml, pyproject.toml and every SPDX header.
authors:
  - given-names: "Eduardo"
    family-names: "Campos-Chávez"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6416-5408"
    affiliation: "Mondragón Lab, Northwestern University"
    email: "bioedca@u.northwestern.edu"
  - given-names: "Alfonso"
    family-names: "Mondragon"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0423-6323"
    affiliation: "Mondragón Lab, Northwestern University"
  # Entity author. CFF 1.2.0 entities accept `name`/`email` but NOT `affiliation`
  # (a person-only field) — carrying one here is what made this file fail schema
  # validation before the named authors were added.
  - name: "The Tether Authors"
    email: "bioedca@u.northwestern.edu"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/bioedca/tether"
url: "https://github.com/bioedca/tether"
keywords:
  - single-molecule FRET
  - smFRET
  - TIRF microscopy
  - trace extraction
  - HMM idealization
  - provenance
  - napari
  - PySide6
# Zenodo archiving (issue #162, maintainer decision of 2026-07-27). The ordering is
# load-bearing and not recoverable after the fact:
#   1. The Zenodo-GitHub integration must be ON before a release tag is pushed. Zenodo
#      only archives releases published while its webhook is active; a release published
#      with it off is not archived, and only re-publishing it while it is still the newest
#      release fixes it. It was switched on 2026-10-11.
#   2. A DOI cannot be reserved through the integration: Zenodo mints each version's DOI
#      when the GitHub Release is published, after the tagged commit exists. So no tagged
#      commit can name its own version DOI, and none is written here.
#   3. `doi:` below is the *concept* DOI, which always resolves to the newest archived
#      version. It never changes between releases, and README.md and docs/citing.md
#      carry the same value. Each version DOI lives on its Zenodo record only.
#   4. The release commit sets `version:` (the tag without its leading "v") and
#      `date-released:` (ISO 8601) to the release being cut; until then they describe the
#      newest archived release.
# docs/release.md "Archiving (Zenodo)" is the operational copy of these steps.
version: "1.0.0-rc2"
date-released: "2026-10-11"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23306397"
# Keep this file's author list identical to any JOSS paper.md author list.
